// Test: a function that lives in a module IS a first-class value — GAP-ZYB-005,
// which said the opposite and was wrong.
//
// The finding read "a module function is not a first-class value; a local one
// is", and concluded that in a modular application 90% of the functions cannot
// be passed. What it had actually found is that `b::doble` is not an expression
// — and that is not a limit on functions, it is what `::` MEANS. `::` is the
// call syntax for a module function (`module::function(args)`); it is not a
// member access that yields a value, because a module is not a class and
// `b::doble` is not a method reference. It is a call with its arguments
// missing, and the parser says so: `expected '(' for module function call`.
//
// Everything the finding wanted works, and this file is the proof. It is the
// fourth entry in that log to fail the same way — GAP-007, 010 and 011 were
// also true statements about the operator NEXT TO the one that answers.
//
// What remains true, and is much smaller: naming a module's function from
// OUTSIDE costs the module an exported accessor. A capability question became
// an ergonomics one.
<# ./funcion_de_modulo_valor_m => b

aplicar(f, v) { <~ f(v) }
local(x) { <~ x + 1 }

// ── the baseline the finding compared against ───────────────────────────────
>> "local como valor      " aplicar(local, 5) ¶

// ── a module hands its own function out, and it is a real function ──────────
h = b::dame()
>> "devuelta por el módulo" h(5) ¶
>> "su tipo               " (h#?)[1] ¶
>> "pasada a una HOF      " aplicar(h, 5) ¶

// ── and it travels in every direction across the boundary ───────────────────
>> "lambda hacia el módulo" b::aplica_aqui((x -> x + 100), 5) ¶
>> "local hacia el módulo " b::aplica_aqui(local, 5) ¶
>> "ida y vuelta          " b::aplica_aqui(b::dame(), 5) ¶

// ── inside the module the bare name needs no ceremony at all ────────────────
// This is the case that matters most, and the one the finding never tried: a
// module's own generic code injecting its own operation.
>> "HOF interna al módulo " b::interno(5) ¶

// Two calls to the accessor hand out THE SAME function, not two copies of it.
// This line could not be written when the file was first recorded: comparing
// two functions had no defined answer, and asking would have decided by
// accident what nobody had decided. It is BUG-ZYB-012, settled since —
// `==` on a function is identity — so the question now has one.
otra = b::dame()
>> "otra vez, y funciona  " otra(5) ¶
>> "y es la misma función " (h == otra) ¶
